# core/views - Which diagrams exist, and how big are they?
#
# Answers: every view resource, its viewpoint, its model, and how many nodes it
# places.
#
# Does NOT prove: quality or currency. A large diagram is not a good one, and a
# view with no nodes is usually an empty diagram somebody left in the model.
#
# Node counts come from the views graph and are OPTIONAL, so a view whose geometry
# was dropped - the converters can emit a no-views or no-geometry profile - still
# appears. Its count is 0, NOT unbound: COUNT over an unmatched OPTIONAL is zero by
# definition, and this header used to promise an unbound column, which is a promise a
# consumer can write a null check against and never see fire. Read 0 as "no nodes were
# published for this view", which is not the same as "this diagram is empty" - and
# core/inventory-summary will say whether the dataset has a views graph at all.
#
# The model comes from {{MEMBERSHIP:view}}. A view is an arch:ModelConcept like any
# other, so membership applies to it unchanged; the previous hardcoded dct:isPartOf
# was the folder edge, which reaches the model for some notations and stops short for
# others.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?view ?viewLabel ?viewpoint ?model (COUNT(DISTINCT ?node) AS ?nodes)
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    ?view a {{ROLE:view_class}} .
    OPTIONAL { ?view {{PATH:label}} ?viewLabel }
    OPTIONAL { ?view {{ROLE:conforms_to_viewpoint}} ?viewpoint }
    OPTIONAL { {{MEMBERSHIP:view}} }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  OPTIONAL {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:views}}
      ?node {{ROLE:view_ref}} ?view .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
}
GROUP BY ?view ?viewLabel ?viewpoint ?model
ORDER BY DESC(?nodes) ?viewLabel
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
